Title: "Labeled Expressions"
Id: "98edd056-5d9f-45f0-a8e8-16c63a2a7fcd"

Text: "A labeled formulation attaches a label to a grouped expression, statement, or specification.

```mlg-fragment
(x + y)[:sum:]
(.x + y.)[:normalized:]
(x = x)[:ref_label:]
```

Only a grouped formulation may carry trailing label tokens directly, and the wrapper may hold a declaration or specification as well as an expression:

```mlg-fragment
(.x is \real.)[:typed:]
(.*' := `*`.)[:operation:]
```

### Single Label per Section of Code

A section of code can have at most one label. Chained labels (`[:a:][:b:]`) and comma-separated multi-labels (`[:a, b:]`) are not permitted:

```mlg-fragment
(x + y)[:step1:]
```

### Referencing Labels in `Justification:`, `References:`, and Prose

A label `[:label:]` marks a piece of code so that other sections can refer to it using `(:label:)`:

- **`Justification:`**: An entry `. for: (:label:) / have: ...` discharges the labeled formulation via the entry's `asserting:` items; an unmatched label is checked inline as usual.
- **`References:`**: An entry `. for: (:label:) / entry: $resource` cites one or more bibliographic resources for the labeled formulation or labeled prose quote.
- **Prose (`OpenText`)**: Standalone `(:label:)` in prose refers to a defined label in the enclosing item.

Single-line formulations may also receive a line-level label using `. [label]: ...` under section headings, and structural groups may carry a `[label]` heading."
Id: "efe841b5-44ed-45f8-8d20-1aacb7fc9bdb"
